=>Introduction:
  Assembly language (in the context of the 8085 microprocessor) is a low-level programming language 
  used to write programs that directly control the operations of the Intel 8085 microprocessor.
  Assembly language is a human-readable version of machine language.
  Instead of writing binary codes like 10101010, we use mnemonics (short words).
  For example:
    MOV → Move data
    ADD → Add
    SUB → Subtract
    LDA → Load accumulator
    These are few commonly used mnemonics.
=>Key components of 8085 assembly language
  =>Mnemonics
    Short instructions (e.g., MOV, ADD, INR) 
  =>Registers
    Internal storage locations of 8085:
    A (Accumulator)
    B, C, D, E, H, L
  =>Operands
    Data or registers used in instructions
    Example: MOV A, B → A and B are operands
  =>Labels & Addresses
    Used for jumps and memory locations
=>Example
  A = 10
  B = 20
  C = A + B
  
  MVI A, 0AH   ; Load 10 into A
  MVI B, 14H   ; Load 20 into B
  ADD B        ; A = A + B
  STA 2000H    ; Store result in memory
  HLT          ; Stop
